Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:01.510 align:start position:0%
 
perhaps one of the most confusing  aspects around the new marvel's  adventures game is uh  what kind of game is it exactly like how  much of it is single player versus  multiplayer  how does the game's progression work and  is it really just destiny 2 with a  marvel skin on it  this can be rather unclear to the  average person if you haven't been  keeping up with developer live streams  we got early access to the game's beta  and we're finally starting to get a  clear picture  and yeah it's actually a lot like  destiny  let's get the biggest question out of  the way yes game does have a single  player campaign  as you play through the campaign you  unlock more of the game's content and  features like new mission types heroes  and multiplayer  hero missions are single player only and  these are where you're gonna get most of  the game story and cut scenes  in them you are given specific heroes to  play and do unique missions to continue  the game's main story  in the beta you play as hulk and kamala  khan  who enter a secret aim facility in  search of jarvis  the campaign is a purely single-player  experience and  at least in the beta the missions are  not repeatable  after the completion of a few hero  missions in the beta you go to the  chimera  an avengers helicarrier and the game's  social space  early on the chimera is mostly abandoned  with only bruce and kamala there and the  rest is all locked off  presumably the camaro will fill up with  more characters and functions as you  progress through the game's story  it's essentially the hub where you'll go  between missions  which leads us to the most important  part of the chimera the war table which  is how you pick your next objective and  access the multiplayer the war table is  broken up  by environmental zones that populate  with different mission types  the largest of these missions are called  war zones  these are multi-objective missions where  you move across several locations  for example this war zone in the pacific  northwest starts you out in an open  forest before eventually leading you  into a small  underground aim facility the missions  are fairly straightforward but there are  small opportunities to deviate off the  main path to find  chess beat up tough enemies or break  into depots for bonus  loot where war zones are meant to be  longer more involved missions for those  looking for something quick you have  drop zones these are bite-sized versions  of war zones with only a single  objective  and they take around 5 to 10 minutes to  complete war zones and drop zones are  meant to be repeatable with multiple  difficulties  you start to see a lot of repeating  environments and objective types so  don't expect these to be as handcrafted  as the campaign missions  that said you will get some more unique  war zones such as condition green where  you're recommended but not required to  play as the hulk  or ones with a boss fight at the end  there's one more mission type in the  beta and those are the  harm emissions these take place in a  combat simulation room  aboard the chimera and are wave based  combat challenges  war zones drop zones and harm missions  are all multiplayer focused with  options to match make or invite your  friends however you can play  everything single player if you want  bringing ai companions with you instead  these ai will use the gear and skills  you've acquired for them  so if you decide to play purely single  player you're going to want to maintain  levels of multiple heroes  there are three progression systems for  every hero  character level power level and  challenge card  character levels are the most  straightforward as their purpose is to  provide you with a new skill point for  each level  you then use these skill points to  unlock a new skill  power level is an indicator of your  stats every piece of gear has a power  level on it and the average of all of  them  is your power level if you've played  destiny then  it's exactly like that game's power  system  gear can also be boosted and with each  boost it increases the power level of  that gear  doing so requires using one of the game  several resources and which resource it  is  depends on the gear uncommon gear can be  boosted 3 times  rare gear 5 times and epic and legendary  gear  10 times equipment like epic gear will  have multiple perks on them  but you'll need to boost it in order to  unlock the perks  last are challenge cards which are  basically battle passes  they go all the way up to level 40 and  each level unlocks cosmetics like  outfits name plates and emotes  or some extra resources you earn points  towards your challenge card by  completing daily and weekly  challenges the challenge cards in the  beta don't offer any sort of paid option  with everything being labeled as  free but there is an empty space for  what is  likely a paid track and yes every hero  has their own individual challenge card  based on our time with the beta this  appears to be the core loop of marvel's  avengers  you have a single player campaign  intermixed with repeatable multiplayer  missions  with the chimera acting as your hub the  progression system seems designed to  keep you playing  long after the story is finished but  until we get our hands on the full  release it's hard to say what avengers  end game will look like if you don't  have access to the beta and want to know  how it actually felt to play the darn  thing  well go check out our impressions video  with jordan phil and myself  and for more on avengers stay tuned to  game spot  wow

